  "sections": {
    "BASICS": {
      "present": true,
      "status": "content",
      "content_blocks": [],
      "subsections": {
        "OVERVIEW": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "OVERVIEW",
          "canonical_heading": "OVERVIEW",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Heterobilharzia americanumis a trematode of the genus Schistosoma that has fresh water lymnaeid snails as intermediate hosts and raccoons as the natural definitive host.",
              "pdf_page": 301,
              "printed_page": 219,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Eggs passed in the feces of raccoons hatch to release miracidia that penetrate freshwater snail hosts. After a period of development and asexual multiplication, the snails release cercariae that infect the next host by skin penetration. After penetrating skin, larvae undergo a migration to the lung and then make their way to mesenteric veins, where separate males and females form pairs. Eggs laid by female worms are carried to the intestinal wall, where they erode their way through to the lumen to be passed in the feces. Other eggs are carried to the liver or other organs by the bloodstream, where they lodge and cause granulomatous disease.",
              "pdf_page": 301,
              "printed_page": 219,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Dogs become infected in contact with freshwater containing cercariae.",
              "pdf_page": 301,
              "printed_page": 219,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Endemic in raccoons in southeastern United States; canine cases reported from Arkansas, Indiana, Florida, Georgia, Kansas, Louisiana, North Carolina, Oklahoma, and Texas.",
              "pdf_page": 301,
              "printed_page": 219,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
        "DEFINITION": {
          "present": false,
